Subject: Convert paths with Boost.Filesystem's UTF-8 codecvt facet on Linux without glibc

Boost.Filesystem builds its path locale on Linux as std::locale(""). On
musl, libstdc++ uses its generic locale model, whose codecvt converts
through the C library's current locale whatever the C++ locale's name,
and that is "C" until the program calls setlocale, so the characters of
a UTF-8 path above U+007F would not be decoded. Linux without glibc now
gets the UTF-8 codecvt facet the BSDs, macOS, Solaris and Haiku get; file
names there are UTF-8 in any case.

--- a/libs/filesystem/src/path.cpp
+++ b/libs/filesystem/src/path.cpp
@@ -31,7 +31,8 @@
 #elif defined(macintosh) || defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__APPLE_CC__) || \
     defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__OpenBSD__) || defined(__DragonFly__) || defined(__NETBSD__) || defined(__NetBSD__) || \
     defined(sun) || defined(__sun) || \
-    defined(__HAIKU__)
+    defined(__HAIKU__) || \
+    (defined(__linux__) && !defined(__GLIBC__))
 // "All BSD system functions expect their string parameters to be in UTF-8 encoding
 // and nothing else." See
 // http://developer.apple.com/mac/library/documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPInternational/Articles/FileEncodings.html
@@ -59,6 +60,10 @@
 //
 // On Solaris 11.4, std::locale("") fails even if LANG is set correctly in the environment.
 // Recent versions of Solaris seem to have transitioned to UTF-8 for filename encoding.
+//
+// On Linux without glibc, libstdc++ uses its generic locale model, whose codecvt
+// converts through the C library's current locale ("C" until the program calls
+// setlocale) whatever name std::locale("") was given; filenames there are UTF-8.
 #include <boost/filesystem/detail/utf8_codecvt_facet.hpp>
 #define BOOST_FILESYSTEM_DETAIL_USE_UTF8_CODECVT_FACET
 #endif
